1 OCCU
1 TITL U.S. Rep.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Einar Hoidale was a U.S. Representative from Minnesota; born in Tromso, Norway, August 17, 1870; immigrated in 1879 to the United States with his parents, who settled near Dawson, Lac qui Parle County, Minnesota; a
2 CONC ttended the common schools; was graduated from the law department of the University of Minnesota at Minneapolis in 1898; was admitted to the bar the same year and commenced practice in New Ulm, Minnesota; prosecuting attorney of B
2 CONC rown County 1900-1906; also engaged as a newspaper publisher at Dawson and Madison, Minnesota, 1900-1904; judge advocate of the State militia 1900-1908; moved to Minneapolis, Minnesota, in 1907 and continued the practice of law; d
2 CONC elegate to the Democratic National Conventions in 1920, 1932, and 1936; unsuccessful Democratic candidate for election to the United States Senate in 1930; elected as a Democrat to the Seventy-third Congress (March 4, 1933-January
2 CONC  3, 1935); was not a candidate for renomination in 1934, but was an unsuccessful candidate for election to the United States Senate; returned to Minneapolis, Minn., and practiced law; died in St. Petersburg, Florida., December 5, 
2 CONC 1952; interment in Lakewood Cemetery, Minneapolis, Minn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Einar Hoidale was born at Tromsø in Troms, Norway. He was the son of Andrew Hoidale and Dorthea (Lund) Hoidale. He immigrated to the United States with his family at the age of seven. From 1890, he served as editor of the Western 
2 CONC Guard newspaper in Madison, Minnesota. He graduated from the University of Minnesota Law School in 1898. He was admitted to the State of Minnesota bar the same year. He commenced practice in New Ulm, Minnesota with Henry Northrup 
2 CONC Somsen (1875-1955). He subsequently served as prosecuting attorney for Brown County, Minnesota from 1900-1906. From 1900-1908, he also served a judge advocate for the Minnesota State Militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the Democratic Party candidate for the United States Senate from Minnesota in 1930. He was elected at-large to the United States House of Representatives and served in the 73rd Congress from 1933 to 1935. He lost a subseque
2 CONC nt election after being nominated again for the U.S. Senate. In both of his Senate campaigns, Hoidale was involved in competitive 3-way races with Republican Party and Farmer Labor Party candidates, and came in second place both t
2 CONC imes. In 1930, he was barely defeated by Republican incumbent Thomas Schall, ahead of former Congressman and future senator Ernest Lundeen of the Farmer Labor Party. In 1934, he lost to Farmer Labor incumbent Henrik Shipstead. 
2 CONT
2 CONT US Congressman. Elected to represent Minnesota as At-large in the United States House of Representatives, serving from 1933 to 1935. Also served as a Democratic Candidate for United States Senator from Minnesota in 1930. He died i
2 CONC n St. Petersburg, Florida and was buried at Lakewood Cemetery in Minneapolis, Minnesota.
