2 CONT * These men came onboard in Montevideo on Oct. 9-1944 (some of them were whalers who had been without a job in Montevideo for some time, and had joined the ship in order to get to the UK).
2 CONT
2 CONT ** The stokers Ebbe Lundberg and George Nichols appear to have been signed on just before departure on Oct. 10 because the regular stokers T. Normann and someone by the last name of Annerfeldt had gone ashore the night before and 
2 CONC had not returned in time for departure.
2 CONT
2 CONT The maritime hearings were held in New York on Nov. 10 and Nov. 13-1944. The following were questioned: The captain, the 3 engineers and the assistant, the 1st and 2 mates, the repairman, Mechanic Petersen, Oiler Holst, Stoker Nic
2 CONC hols, and Mechanic Travers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jon Veiberg's daughter recently contacted me to tell me her father shared a cabin with my father on D/S Ringulv. She also told me about the book (Jon's Odyssey), which has several passages about, and pictures of, my father in it. 
2 CONC (It can be purchased through the Internet, for instance at Amazon.com). Veiberg also writes about his voyages on M/T Elise, M/S Høegh Silverlight and D/S Rio Branco, all listed on this website.
2 CONT
2 CONT Related external links:
2 CONT
2 CONT More on the 3 Norwegians - (The first one listed, Ordinary Seaman Torgny Jorde, died in an accident on board on Jan. 19-1943). Lars Teien is listed as Engineer at this memorial for seamen in Stavern, Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bombay Explosion - Details of the events and marvellous pictures.
2 CONT
2 CONT En annen radiotelegrafist som seilte med Einar har skrevet bok: Her er noen utdrag. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Radio Officers, Leaving England;
2 CONT
2 CONT Our group traveled by train to Cardiff in the Bristol Channel, Wales. We were to be passengers on a Norwegian motor ship, the M/S Tai Shan, about 9000 tons. She had cabin space for twelve passengers, among them a Norwegian doctor 
2 CONC and his wife as nurse. They were to open a medical office for Seamen at Bombay. our group was distributed among the regular crew. I wound up among the gun crews in quarters constructed below the regular aft crew quarters. I consol
2 CONC ed myself it was a temporary setback, sort of, because generally a Radio Officer at ‘worst’ would only have to share with one other Radio Officer. Usually he would have his own cabin, often on the same deck as the Captain’s quarte
2 CONC rs, or on the bridge next to the radio station. (I briefly wondered if I already was taking on airs of having taken a step ‘above’ the regular ship crew?).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tai Shan sailed up the Irish Sea to join a convoy bound for Gibraltar sailing out into the Atlantic, north of Ireland just before Christmas. Any notion of aforementioned airs succumbed to my old friend, seasickness, never mind
2 CONC  mal-the-mer! Because this was, again, Atlantic winter-time-seasickness, numbing, relentless, two day’s worth...
2 CONT
2 CONT I remembered my father’s admonishing about the unforgiving sea, again. Despite our almost passenger status we were asked to pitch in doing lookout duty, one hour at a time. One of our number was uncomfortable climbing up to the cr
2 CONC ow’s nest and would elect to spend two hours so as not having to climb up twice. I was curious about why the height did not bother me, so far as the seasickness was concerned; it was wintertime and even one hour as a lookout was a
2 CONC  cold time. And the movement of the ship was accentuated at that height; perhaps it was better to leave well enough alone...
2 CONT
2 CONT This was my third convoy. Don’t know how many ships were in the convoy; we seemed to have ample Navy support. The look-out post from the crow’s nest offered a panoramic view, albeit a cold one. So it wasn’t exactly, deja vue. Beca
2 CONC use of the German airforce operating from Brest in France the convoy took a wide westward swing before heading east towards Gibraltar. We were to arrive there just after Christmas, enjoying the festivities prepared for that holida
2 CONC y at sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was my fourth Christmas away from Norway. But a lot had happened in many different places to this young Norwegian and I seemed to remember it was almost difficult to envision what could be happening at home in Odda. At least I 
2 CONC was where the Holiday was being remembered and taken note of. The previous Christmas was, as noted, my lost Christmas.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were three English women in the Steward’s department, two in their low twenties, one probably -thirty. They were occupied with the Officers’ messroom duties. The older girl seemed to act as sort of the mother hen. It was jok
2 CONC ed about by the rest of the crew, perhaps likely envy, that the ‘old’ girl couldn’t always be around...
2 CONT
2 CONT I was on lookout duty on top of the bridge ‘house’, there were railings, during the Christmas Eve party for the Officers in the Captain’s quarters. In the dusky darkness I could see someone taking one of the young girls through th
2 CONC e area separating the mid-ship structures to his cabin. But that was not the area I was supposed to be watching... (I was surprised they wouldn’t be missed... ) My watch relief came and I joined the crews’ festivities; the Captain
2 CONC  was making his holiday rounds to the crews’ quarters. (Gallantly?, I "stored" my observation).
2 CONT
2 CONT The convoy turned in to Gibraltar where we would wait for a convoy through the Mediterranean. The anchorage was near the Spanish coast. One got used to noise from small depth charges the English detonated at regular intervals. Mot
2 CONC or launches would cruise slowly among the ships at anchor and drop these small depth charges in order to deter people from swimming out from the Spanish coast to attach underwater bombs to the stabilizing keels of ships at anchor.
2 CONC  Several ships had been sunk in that manner.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Spanish area called "La Linnea", bordering Gibraltar, could be clearly observed from the Tai Shan. La Linea was fully lit at night, street traffic observable. Gibraltar under a wartime blackout. We were also close enough to ob
2 CONC serve much of the Naval comings and goings at Gibraltar proper. Two halves of a Norwegian tank ship could be seen ‘tied’ together at a pier, borrowing field glasses on the bridge. We were told the ship broke into two halves when t
2 CONC orpedoed and the two halves were towed to Gibraltar. The two halves at were to be joined again when drydock space would become available; apparently Navy ship repairs were presently a priority.
2 CONT
2 CONT I remembered having read about tank ships breaking apart at the pump room just aft of the mid-ship. The pump room being the weakest section of the ship, a room from the bottom deck, as wide as the ship, all the way to the main dec
2 CONC k and about 8 feet along the ship. This is were the pumps are located, electric or steam, used to pump the oil ashore, which same pumps also are used to pump sea water into the tanks for ballast.
2 CONT
2 CONT One incident about "our" gun crew must be mentioned. One of them, a Danish fellow taller than I at 6 foot one, thought a Spanish rowboat was passing too close to our ship and motioned the rowboat away. One of the two in the rowboa
2 CONC t called back: -"this Espania!" The Dane lifted his rifle and, to my surprise, fired. The bullet split the Spaniards’ port oar. They, thinking better of it, hurriedly rowed away with their oar and a half. The gun crew Officer was 
2 CONC supportive, in a sense; may be the Spaniards needed a lesson...
2 CONT
2 CONT War at sea, again:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tai Shan joined a convoy of some forty ships sailing eastward toward the Suez Canal. There seemed to be an abundance of escort ships. One rebuilt light cruiser I thought almost looked like a porcupine because of the number ant
2 CONC i aircraft guns. There were several batteries of so-called "Pom-Pom" guns, four or six barrels in a battery. When they were fired, the barrels would move up and down in sequence, hence it was nicknamed ", the Chicago Piano" by the
2 CONC  British. During the first of two air attacks, maybe three, this particular ship fairly looked like she was ablaze with all her batteries firing away.
2 CONT
2 CONT One German bomber was brought down ahead of the convoy during the first attack at about dusk in cloudy weather. A life jacket with its small light floated in the middle of the convoy as we passed by. It was too dark to see if it a
2 CONC lso held anyone. Nevertheless, one of the ships opened fire at the life jacket, other ships joined in, and soon tracer bullets could be seen bouncing off the water causing everyone on the other ships to duck till orders to cease f
2 CONC iring were issued and relayed. "Damned idiots", I uttered about those who would fire at, even an enemy, someone helpless in a life jacket. The firing likely was due to someone nervously firing first, then others joining in, perhap
2 CONC s not to be left out.
2 CONT
2 CONT I had been asked to join one of the gunners on watch in the port side pill box for a 20mm Oerlican, (anti-air craft gun). My duty was to change cartridge magazines, about 8-9 inches wide and about 12 inches diameter. But this neve
2 CONC r happened because our Captain issued orders not to fire unless an airplane was seen approaching our ship. I wondered how this could be possible with darkness approaching. The gunner announced he wanted a cigarette. To light it he
2 CONC  had me bend over him to make sure the light from the match was not visible, also cupping his hands. He was about my age and had gone to gunnery school because he liked duty as armed guard, thought the Navy would be too restrictiv
2 CONC e. The position as a gunner apparently offered some status.
2 CONT
2 CONT A guard rail made from about one inch diameter pipe "surrounded" the pill box. It was formed above and half way around the -ship-side of each box, about two and a half foot high. The gunner advised this was to prevent the barrel o
2 CONC f the gun from traversing the ship, and the gunner from shooting down his own rigging in case he followed an aircraft flying across the ship. This apparently had happened during fervor of following the airplane, hence the guard ra
2 CONC il.
2 CONT
2 CONT I recalled one of the fellows at the radio school who had been partly invalided serving on a British corvette sunk while guarding a convoy to Malta. Why he was on an English Navy ship did not come up. His injuries would prevent hi
2 CONC m from serving on a ship in war time.
2 CONT
2 CONT All the convoys bound for Malta and beyond had to pass through the narrow waters between the Italian island Pantelleria and Tunis, a strait about 50 statute miles wide. German bombers operating from both sides, made this a veritab
2 CONC le gauntlet if there ever was one. To supply Malta under those conditions was an almost unbelievable feat. The stories of the frequency of bombing attacks on Valetta, the capital of Malta, sounded like legions in numbers...
2 CONT
2 CONT Tai Shan also served as the convoy Vice Commodore Operation’s ship. The Vice Commodore’s Chief Petty Officer had made 40 runs between Gibraltar and Malta and back. He was a slim, intense, Englishman about five foot five inches tal
2 CONC l. He seemed to fit the average build of English men borne after World War I. I seemed to have read about this condition brought on by scarcity of food, etc. To me his many trips to Malta and back seemed like an attempt to tempt f
2 CONC ate.... ; there was hardly room for any more service ribbons on this man’s jacket sleeve.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ships of the Commodore’s section of the convoy separated as we neared the route to Malta. The rest of the convoy proceeded towards the Suez Canal without further incidents. The Tai Shan had proceeded to anchorage at the northe
2 CONC rn end of the Suez Canal, Port Said, to pick up a pilot and await our turn to proceed southward. Adventure, the word seemed to become - shopworn. Yet this was the Port Said of many lurid stories and films. The war-time aura did no
2 CONC t change this much despite the Navy ships and war materiel being unloaded from cargo ships.
2 CONT
2 CONT While waiting to get clearance to proceed southward Egyptian boats came alongside with a member of the crew ‘sitting’ in the mast hawking wares and sundry tourist type items. Among them were aphrodisiacs which the doctor going to 
2 CONC Bombay shook his head about, both because he said were ineffective, and likely unsanitary. Getting underway we noticed the anti aircraft batteries along both sides of the canal, interspersed with dummy tanks and gun emplacements. 
2 CONC But the war itself seemed removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ship had to anchor up in the so-called Bitter Lake, about two thirds way through to let northbound, perhaps of higher priority shipping, pass. I never learned the origin of the name of the lake, maybe the water had once been b
2 CONC itter when it was an inland lake... The port of Suez just seemed like a row of houses, all white, with some minarets of worship places at intervals. Business seemed to go on as usual though there was also a lot of military traffic
2 CONC  moving to and fro, some place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mount Sinai, of Moses and Ten Commandment fame, became a subject before leaving the canal; don’t recall if the mountain was visible. At 24 it likely was not an important subject, except in passing (by), as it were.
2 CONT
2 CONT Svein and I were always eager for some exercise and the Second Mate turned up two pairs of boxing gloves. He was surprised when we two took to sparring on the poop deck, often to everyone’s amusement. Svein was declared the eventu
2 CONC al winner because I wound up with a ‘shiner’ blooming into a black eye.
2 CONT
2 CONT The young girls, likely just about my age, were asking if I felt badly about ‘loosing’; explaining it was a sparring exercise only was a lost effort... I thought I had detected an ambiguous interest by the crew about women on a ca
2 CONC rgo ship, especially in a war-time situation. Oddly, the Danish gunner seemed defensive about the girls when it was expressed they had no place on a ship because of the war, placing them at perils with extra efforts required in em
2 CONC ergencies. A precursor to arguments about women in service, 45 years later? The girls did compliment me on my English, which was something, anyway, somehow. Evidently the extra, even fleeting attention, was OK. I kept my distance,
2 CONC  though, in case it should rub some Officer the wrong way; we were newly turned out Radio Officers, there was a hierarchy among some of the regular Officers...
2 CONT
2 CONT We were glad to have the continuing job as look-outs although once into the Red Sea the look-out position seemed to become less of a necessity. I had become friendly with the Chief Radio Officer; the ship also had two English Radi
2 CONC o Officers. The two groups, the sparks on the ship and the passenger ‘sparks’, enjoyed each other’s company. It offered a break from a usual routine. The resident sparks, was something of a genius to me. He was an old-time sparks 
2 CONC and his evenings were occupied with repairing sundry electrical items, such as rewinding the armatures on broken electric fans. My mind was like a sponge taking everything in, salting it away. It might come in handy some day in th
2 CONC e future.
2 CONT
2 CONT The resident spark’s routine was of long standing, many years at sea, which easily was outside my future plans. Having read a lot about Seamen’s lives at sea, I had already perceived a type of connection between lonely cowboy type
2 CONC  songs and songs I remembered as lonely Seamen type songs before I left Norway. The resident sparks was like a long time employee of the Wilhelmsen Line, owners of the Tai Shan, one of the biggest shipping companies in Norway. At 
2 CONC 24, again, that prospect was definitely not a part of my future...
2 CONT
2 CONT I was keenly aware that I reveled in this new experience; I felt I had just breezed through the school. And the ‘now’ was like a continuing change. At my age I was, like any young man, just looking straight ahead; the world -my oy
2 CONC ster, which expression I had as yet not come across, even. The adventures on the horizon, sure to be there, were like a promise; fifty years later the expression, -like a high-, would come into vogue, perhaps defining my feelings,
2 CONC  in -44....
2 CONT
2 CONT Our first port of call in India was the port of Cochin, almost at the southern end of this huge peninsula. It did not really look like a port. The ship was tied up to buoys, fore and aft, in a river with a lot of forest around. Li
2 CONC ghters came along side to take the cargo destined for this place. The Captain did us a favor by letting us draw money on our standby salaries, because we were not really part of his crew. (The resident Radio Officer informed me of
2 CONC  this and I told him we really appreciated that). The Captain had learned that Svein and I both had been prisoners, Svein by the Germans, on a "fraider" and I in North Africa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Svein’s story was the far more adventurous. A seaplane from a German raider had torn their antenna off with a hook device and had sunk their ship after they had been ordered off, taking the crew prisoners. This had taken place in 
2 CONC the Indian Ocean. (After one or two incidents with seas planes ripping off the antennas, British "aerials", ships were rigged with reserve antennas below the mast level to allow sending of SOS with position of the raider). ‘Svein’
2 CONC s’ raider in turn was intercepted by an English warship and the crews were freed. Svein also had gotten me interested in trying to get a ship sailing to Australia, (which never happened). He would relate stories about having visit
2 CONC ed that fascinating land, having had opportunity to visit a ‘sheep station’ on an excursion. Cochin really was no place to visit and spend our money. A hotel, "some place in the woods", with a lot of waiters was the only diversion
2 CONC . Not interested in drinking the passenger sparks soon repaired to the ship anxious to get on to Bombay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Except for the almost three months onboard the Tordenskjold I had been unemployed for some time, since we were taken off Ringulv at Safi, North Africa. I was anxious to become useful. Of course, a lot of sailors would say that the
2 CONC  Radio Officer’s job, or position, if you will, still was not work...
2 CONT
2 CONT Bombay, India;
2 CONT
2 CONT Arriving at Bombay we shook hands with the crew all around thanking them for the hospitality bestowed on us. We were quartered at a small Norwegian rest type place, with room for about 12 guests. (The expression R&R was unknown to
2 CONC  us, at that time). We were served hand and foot by Indian servants, the ‘hotel’ being run by a Norwegian former missionary couple. There was an esplanade in front of the house, facing part of the ocean, or bay, with similar housi
2 CONC ng along for a distance. During our stay there we could watch American ‘GIs’ practicing formation and parade drills on this convenient esplanade.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Norwegian shipping office provided us with necessary cash; we were employed now, just waiting for duty. It was necessary to visit the Police Headquarters to obtain a permission pass to stay ashore while awaiting ship duty. The
2 CONC  Police Chief’s uniform was that of an Army colonel’s. (He was English).
2 CONT
2 CONT We were called in, one by one. He had a folder in front of him. It seemed to me after a while, that he had more information about my whereabouts since I left Norway than I remembered; even about my stay in North Africa. He correct
2 CONC ed me a couple of times as I detailed where and when I had been, here and there. But he was very friendly and wished these young people good luck in their future "endeavor".
2 CONT
2 CONT It was a big city, diversions depended on what one was looking for. A modern movie house was air conditioned, our first experience with that system. But customers were warned to bring a jacket, apparently the system only worked fu
2 CONC ll on, with the result that when one finally left, the outside heat made it feel like walking into an oven. One couldn’t wait to get to the hotel to try and take a shower. Dining at restaurants was expensive for our taste, caterin
2 CONC g mostly to the better off, it seemed.
2 CONT
2 CONT We were advised about a good size swimming pool located near our hotel ; soon a lot of our time was spent there, in the English section of the city. I daringly tried the 10 meter dive which the others said I managed, after a fashi
2 CONC on; they thought as divers go I needed a lot more practice on the 3 meter board. The missionary felt it was not necessary to be introduced to the race track, but it was there...
2 CONT
2 CONT It was a new for some of us, hence of interest. I wondered briefly how the well-to-do could be there gambling away, as if this was Haut couture, high fashion, 100 rupee notes in their hands, while it seemed that the Indians around
2 CONC  the dock areas were existing on a couple of rupees, (60 cents) a day. I had seen poverty in North Africa, but not on such a pervasive scale as in Bombay. The seeming multitude of people apparently overflowed available housing int
2 CONC o the streets. One wondered where they disappeared to as the monsoon season with driving, unceasing, rain arrived. Discussing this aspect with the former missionary at the hotel, he advised that one ought not to get involved about
2 CONC  local customs or systems. Philosophically, perhaps, one may contemplate a statement from religion, -there will always be the poor around...
2 CONT
2 CONT We were told Mahatma Ghandi was working to improve the lot of the people under the caste system, except it seemed such a huge, unmanageable, task as to "boggle" the mind. The missionary told of examples where the English attempted
2 CONC  to set up a trade training system for the Indians, who generally are quite adept, quickly becoming skilled crafts people. It developed that "these" people could not possibly work or train with "those" people, who were of a differ
2 CONC ent caste or religion. And the numbers of differences often caused abandonment of the venture. There was a story about an attempt by the English wishing to spray ponds and pools of stagnant water with kerosene, while we were there
2 CONC , in order to kill the larva of malaria carrying mosquito. They had to shelve the effort because it almost developed into a holy war due to the varied religious beliefs. It was quite observable that the success of the former Briti
2 CONC sh hegemony in India, now seemingly on the wane, depended on their not mixing in internal affairs of India proper. But forthcoming changes also seemed to be in the offing. War-time brings changes one would otherwise not attempt.
2 CONT
2 CONT The English singing idol, Vera Lynn, came to entertain the British troops. We were able to attend the performance. The songstress came in escorted by a British colonel and the welcome by the troops was overwhelming. This was ‘big 
2 CONC time’ with a singer we just barely had heard about now being almost rioted over by the British Soldiers. The enthusiasm exhibited by the British service men was not unexpected.
2 CONT
2 CONT While attending radio school I remembered being near a hotel just off Leicester Square in London, near the Picadilly Circus as I recall. Clark Gable was staying at this hotel. As he exited from the hotel one evening the street in 
2 CONC front of the hotel was completely blocked by the enthusiastic G.I. service men. Such adoration of performers was generally not a tradition with the Norwegians; perhaps I was unaccustomed to this being from a small town. But I was 
2 CONC surprised to learn that Clark Gable, the actor, was a tail gunner on one of the American bombers. And, I thought it might have been OK to get to see the guy; I was not really that blaze... We did get to gaze at Vera Lynn, though, 
2 CONC and listen to her songs....
2 CONT
2 CONT The time in the Radio Officer pool became adventure time, again. This time it was more like a real paid vacation; the time at school had not been any hardship. I had taken to the school work without much problem. The thoughts abou
2 CONC t relatives in Norway became less frequent, as if a condition it was useless to think much about; the absence of any news, -remote.
2 CONT
2 CONT One Norwegian ship, about 3000 tons, apparently formerly sailing around these waters with Indian crew and Norwegian Officers was ‘laid’ up at anchor in the huge harbor. The Captain and one Indian crew member were ‘tending’ the shi
2 CONC p. The Captain invited the new Radio Officers onboard to see if the radio station could be made operable again, "and to give you something to do", he advised. But the equipment had gone through a period of non-use and corrosion ap
2 CONC peared worse than could be reasonably cleared up in the time we thought available.
2 CONT
2 CONT All the equipment would have to be disassembled, cleaned, and reassembled. We deemed the extant, pervasive, humidity soon would undo the necessary work; the ship was not expected to leave in the foreseeable future. We were not sur
2 CONC e the Captain accepted our assessment even as I noted that the equipment might be left disassembled in case ships came in requiring our service, as intended. We did not really look forward to long, sweaty, work in a cramped radio 
2 CONC station. Besides, we were anxious to become "real" Radio Officers, not repairmen. It would have been a rewarding peace-time job, real on-the-job training.
2 CONT
2 CONT Part of our diversion in this big city was one of several dance halls were one could dance with young girls, mostly Anglo/Indian. They were the results of union by English Soldiers and Indian mothers. Without exception these girls
2 CONC  talked about going ‘home’ to England, a place they had just heard about. I was reminded of a similar condition about 45 years later when England absorbed a lot of individuals from former colonies. The condition of the Amerasian c
2 CONC hildren left behind in Korea and Vietnam by American Soldiers exemplified a similar problem, misfortune even; apparently always a sad reminder, and remainder, of war...
2 CONT
2 CONT The first call for a Radio Officer was on a ship, around 3000 tons, sailing coast-wise around India. Likely because of the visit to the laid up ship not one of us volunteered for the job, it was as if no one could see being tied t
2 CONC o this part of the world... I pulled the -short straw for the job. I never remembered whether I had a stomach problem on the visit to the doctor, going through the pre-hiring check-up, or if this was a convenient condition just co
2 CONC ming up...
2 CONT
2 CONT I thought the heat and the food in this region was my problem, but I often thought later the region itself might have had as much to do with the condition; perhaps a convenient incident? I remember trying to rationalize that the d
2 CONC octor would have seen through that excuse... The result was that another straw routine was taken to fill the job.
2 CONT
2 CONT After about four weeks all the standby sparks had been assigned. Both Sigvart and I had been assigned to the M/S Braganza March 27,-44. She was the former "Pacific Trader" under English flag, now under Norwegian flag. The Chief sp
2 CONC arks about 12 years our senior. She was a big ship, 9900 tons dead weight. As the Pacific Trader she had cabin rooms for about 30 passengers, all on first deck above the main deck, below the deck with the Captain’s quarters. Sigva
2 CONC rt and I were assigned a passenger cabin on the main deck, nicely appointed. Our Chief had been eating with the other Officers. Apparently for lack of space the three of us now took our meals in a smaller 4 man messroom. Sigvart a
2 CONC nd I didn’t mind; if our Chief minded not eating with the other Officers we figured it would be his problem. We had not really gotten into our new position as yet. If there was to be any status change we’d take it in stride. Sigva
2 CONC rt had been to sea for some time, I would follow his take on things. He had a ready smile and easy manner, sure of himself, tall and lanky like me. I trusted his judgment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Braganza had been tied up in the Prince’s dock for engine, or motor, repair, a lube oil leakage problem. The Victoria dock was adjacent. The first dock, arriving at Bombay, was the Alexandria dock. Entrance to these three docks wa
2 CONC s by way of locks because of high and low water conditions and the construction of the harbor generally. Curious about when we would leave, the answer was very soon, which turned out to become three months....
2 CONT
2 CONT One day while Sigvart and I were ashore a killing had occurred onboard the Braganza. A Latvian motor man was always taunting an Estonian second cook. He had been warned to lay off. But one day the Latvian was standing in the galle
2 CONC y doorway threatening the second cook. The Estonian grabbed a large knife and gave chase. The Latvian ran up the first stairway to the mid ship, by the passenger cabins, where the Estonian caught him and ran the knife into his kid
2 CONC ney area. The Latvian died before the medical help arrived. (The wooden deck had markings of being recently cleaned and sanded by the time Sigvart and I came back onboard).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Braganza was still in Bombay as the trial took place, with witnesses from ship and acquaintances from shore. The Estonian was found not guilty; the papers reported self defense as a determining factor. He did not return to the
2 CONC  ship, however, perhaps not re-hired. I did wonder, at times, if maybe the word adventure was being, over-used.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time after the killing the first ship to be assigned a sparks from our group had already made a round trip ‘down the coast, to Goa, Cochin, Colombo on Ceylon, (now Sri Lanka). The ship was tied up at a pier outside the dock a
2 CONC reas because of a part cargo of sulfur. Our friend of course came onboard the Braganza to fill us in about his trip. Sigvart and I were anxious to hear about Goa and Ceylon, exotic places, and the first of our group as a new Radio
2 CONC  Officer. But, India was beginning to wear thin for a long time visit.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ammunition Ship Explosion:
2 CONT
2 CONT Our friend was to experience the closest of escapes in an almost ancillary war experience. So far as we knew Bombay had never experienced an enemy attack of any kind. But an about 1500 tons ammunition explosion was a reminder of a
2 CONC  war condition. The happening was like a totally unexpected enemy attack.
2 CONT
2 CONT The explosion happened within the dock area because of a major miscalculation by the Harbor Master, in our humble opinions. An English ship came from Karachi to Bombay with a cotton cargo on top of about 1500 tons of ammunition. T
2 CONC he cotton was smoldering and her Captain had asked permission to scuttle the ship at the far end of this huge harbor. (It seemed one could "hide’ New York harbor here). He was overruled by the Harbor Master who ordered the ship br
2 CONC ought into the Victoria dock on high water at noon. He had four fire engines brought to the dock side. There was an about 100 foot wide pier span, with a large storage shed, separating the Prince and the Victoria docks. We three r
2 CONC adio people were practicing signaling with handheld signal light in the bridge house, about two PM, when we were advised there was a fire on a ship in the Victoria dock. Getting out on the bridge we saw the flames emanating from t
2 CONC he Englishman’s nr. two hold, virtually like a giant blow torch. The four fire engines were valiantly directing streams of water from their hoses into this enormous blaze. It appeared useless, the flames evaporating the water inst
2 CONC antly.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ammunition blew up around 1600 hours, (wartime hours), flattening people on the Braganza’s deck, we ducked behind the bridge front. The fire engines were never seen again, nor their people. Luckily, for us, the main explosion 
2 CONC spent its force against the about 100-foot wide stone pier separating the two docks. A ship about 4000 tons, maybe about 200 feet long, immediately ahead, and inside of the exploding ship was lifted sternwise onto the pier, the bo
2 CONC w hanging in the water, stern straight in on the pier, the ship broken at about the midship.
2 CONT
2 CONT The cook on a Norwegian ship, "Belray", tied up between us and the exploding ship had been standing just outside his galley watching. As he recovered from the force of the blast he jumped inside the galley door opening; a piece of
2 CONC  steel caught his hand on the side of the door frame opening severing the hand completely from the wrist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our friend’s ship outside where Braganza laid was set afire, he just barely escaping. The ship’s load of sulfur was set afire by debris from the explosion. (Because sulfur on fire may not be extinguished with water the ship was ab
2 CONC andoned and left to burn out.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Conflagration was everywhere, exploding ammunition flying about. A four foot square, jagged, steel plate fell on our battery box on top of the bridge house and set it afire. This fire was put out and all our crew ordered ashore. T
2 CONC he Second Mate and a couple of A.B.s to remain on watch. As I came down the gangway an Indian was supporting another with part of his shoulder torn open. He asked if I could attempt to drive a flatbed truck abandoned there, and he
2 CONC lp them get away. I told him I had never driven a car or truck, wouldn’t know how to start it even. He thought he could get someone else.
2 CONT
2 CONT On my way out of the dock area I had just reached a gate in the 10ft high brick wall surrounding the harbor, when another explosion let loose. I instinctively dove headlong outside right next to the wall, covering my head. A whirr
2 CONC ing sound up in the air caused me to look up. A 6ft piece of steel, in windmill fashion, was "flying’ towards three huge oil tanks. The steel piece knocked some piping off on top of one of the tanks starting a small fire.
2 CONT
2 CONT I thought later I probably panicked, expecting another problem from oil tanks on fire. (We learned later the tanks had been emptied). On a dead run at once, the nearest street was clogged with Indians of all ages streaming away fr
2 CONC om the dock area. These people, among the poorest of the poor, seemed to be living in the streets around the dock area. A bicycle with the chain off the wheel lay next to an abandoned street car. Hurriedly attaching the chain I to
2 CONC ok off pedaling the bicycle through throngs of people and usual cows roaming the streets, the thought of the fire on the oil tank driving me on. I did not stop till I was on the other side of the town at the small hotel where we h
2 CONC ad stayed arriving from England.
2 CONT
2 CONT I spent the night and took a bus to get back onboard the next forenoon. Devastation was pervasive. We learned later some 600 Indians including the harbor master were killed; 34 ships were damaged, inside and outside the harbor, 16
2 CONC  beyond repair, unofficial. (A few years before writing this story I met a Norwegian former sea Captain at Oslo. He had been First Mate on a ship in the same dock, the Victoria, where the ship blew up. He gave me an article he had
2 CONC  written for a paper describing their valiant, but in vain, effort to save their ship. I came to understand then that our escape was in the nature of extreme fortune, deliverance and miracles being beyond my ken).
2 CONT
2 CONT None of the deck crew had yet arrived onboard as evening approached. I offered to take the watch job that first night. Dusk approaching, I found a coil of rope and sat with my back against the mid-ship bulkhead, eyes wide open, pr
2 CONC actically not moving all night trying to wonder why I had volunteered to be watchman, (wondering later if I had blinked at all)... 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fires were still burning around the dock area and some ammunition was still exploding. It was stated, later, that Indians were bringing 50 lbs gold bars to the port officials during the following days, also from the exploding ship
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The Captain I met at Oslo corrected me on this: one gold bar had fallen on the veranda of Farsi, (Persian), living there and he had brought it to the officials. It was reported later, that the ship had carried 155 gold bars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mahatma Ghandi:
2 CONT
2 CONT A couple of days after the explosion two open limousines passed slowly by ‘our’ dock area We were surprised to spot this famous Indian and other dignitaries reviewing the area and devastation. Mr. Ghandi waved graciously. The limo
2 CONC s almost passed out of sight before the significance of this man’s visit could sink in; we likely did not really understand the impact of this man on his country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our friend shipped out again just about right away. Years later, the expression, "ill-fated" about the Braganza, why did this thought stick in my mind? The thought occurred to me whenever someone mentioned that buying an old car c
2 CONC ould be like taking on someone else’s problem. Perhaps she had been sold because of the engine problem, low oil pressure, for which she was now laid up to be repaired....
2 CONT
2 CONT It crossed my mind, too, we were going through a period of a lot of leisure, with plain just about nothing to do.
2 CONT
2 CONT I knew very well what the radio position entailed, BUT, I’d be doing something worth while, if we were at sea.. Not that I was suffering any conscience pangs; I was anxious, nevertheless, to be where we belonged, at sea more than 
2 CONC in port.
2 CONT
2 CONT Go to: Chapter Sixteen - Radio Officer Duty, finally;
2 CONT
2 CONT Index
2 CONT
2 CONT Chapter Sixteen
2 CONT
2 CONT Radio Officer Duty, finally;
2 CONT
2 CONT The Braganza was declared seaworthy at last. Cargo was taken on for Durban, South Africa. Proceeding southward we became used to an almost daily engine, or motor stop. Dead in the water is not a desirable condition in wartime, ‘th
2 CONC ough this theater of operation seemed to lie between the Japanese and German spheres... The different motion of a ship, no longer under power, seemed as noticeable as the absence of the thump-thump from the motor; the duration usu
2 CONC ally was short.
2 CONT
2 CONT The gun crew on our ship was British, twelve total including the sergeant and corporal. Because we had spent a considerable time in England I found sort of a common ground with the gunners, more to pass my free time. Even after fo
2 CONC ur years now at sea I was surprised by some atrocious language. It seemed that particularly among 2 or 3 out of the twelve, but every third or fourth spoken was what they wished to say, the rest a stream of mostly sexually slanted
2 CONC  invectives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Asking one of them if he didn’t worry about misusing his language among family members, for ex.. the young soldier mostly laughed as if he would say,-"what do you mean.?". Perhaps he was unaware to what extent it had become a habi
2 CONC t; he was likely not yet twenty. After a while though they seemed to tone down their language whenever I engaged them in talks visiting their gun stations.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the other gunners, probably a couple of years older than I, was very accommodating whenever we found time for conversation. I felt I had a real valuable exchange of information about our different backgrounds. He had a vari
2 CONC ed experience in the armed forces of a different country and he appreciated my forthrightness about being on a learning curve, likely not even realizing it. We had many good laughs over "picking one anther’s brain"; differences se
2 CONC em to amalgamate. He laughed outright, good-naturedly, when I attempted to use that expression proving nationalities were not that different. In retrospect, I was learning, anyway.
2 CONT
2 CONT The gunnery sergeant expressed a desire to occupy one of the passenger cabins because of his rank. There had been some problem with one of the younger men not following orders. Their quarters were built over the regular crews’ qua
2 CONC rters on the stern section of the ship. All the former passenger cabins were unused but the Captain apparently felt the Army sergeant would stay with the gunners as agreed to when the group came onboard his ship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our first and only radio message received arrived from London while I was on watch, two days after leaving Bombay. During peace-time the Radio Officer would contact designated shore-to-ship radio station and "receive" messages, al
2 CONC most like by telephone, except by Morse code. Due to the nature of transmitted radio signals, that they are easily detected and the sender ship located by direction finder equipment, radio (transmission) silence was the rule durin
2 CONC g the war. Messages to ships therefore were one way, from a specific shore station to a ship. The Officer on watch would listen to designated radio stations sending "traffic" lists, at scheduled hours, indicating which ships they 
2 CONC had messages for by designated, individual ship’s, call signs. The messages would be sent in code form, usually repeated three times a day. Our message station was London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Braganza’s call sign came up on the traffic list. Waiting for our message to be transmitted, three times as usual to ensure reception, it was copied and decoded using our code books. The message ordered a change in Braganza’s desi
2 CONC gnated code "name"; the change apparently was a usual way of making identification of a ship more difficult, for some unintended source. The change was duly noted in the radio log.
2 CONT
2 CONT I advised our Chief who took the information to the Captain. I was surprised when the Chief came back for the code books and had to sit down with the Captain to verify the decoding of the message. The Chief assured me the Captain 
2 CONC would have gone through the same procedure even if he, the Chief, had received the message. (But there remained a feeling with me that it was really like test-time again. If I had not copied the message correctly it could not have
2 CONC  been decoded; my reasoning. Was I in a defensive mode?). We laughed it off at dinner time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In due time we passed by a small group of islands, (Comoros), on our way through the Mozambique Channel, continuing by the island of Madagascar. I enjoyed having access to the chart room behind the bridge, the radio station situat
2 CONC ed just next door ; one could follow the daily travel of the ship.
2 CONT
2 CONT A bit of history from my travelogue reading days in Norway about Mozambique, once Portuguese East Africa, fits in here. Vasco da Gama, (1460-1524), credited with discovering the trade route to India supposedly came about it this w
2 CONC ay, stealthily like: Native Mozambique spice traders were known to travel to India but refused to divulge their routes. da Gama, the story goes, invited one of the traders to a party on his ship, got him good and drunk. The story 
2 CONC continues with the trader finally sobering up as da Gama’s ship was too far on the way towards India to return to Mozambique and the trader thus had to divulge the rest of the route...
2 CONT
2 CONT Durban, South East Africa;
2 CONT
2 CONT I was sorry to have to bid good bye to one of the gun crew who had to return to Bombay because he was stationed there. As noted above this Englishman was different than most, to me a gentleman, though an enlisted soldier. I had le
2 CONC arned that all Officers were (supposed) gentlemen...
2 CONT
2 CONT Another change in the crew occurred when three Indian mess men signed off in order to get back to India. In their place three Africaners were hired. We were to experience a type cultural change and incident later with one of the A
2 CONC fricaner messmen, somewhat of an eye opener, noted later. (Afrikaner, as we understood it, was of Dutch and native ancestry).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigvart and I soon found a couple of nice dancing areas, more like the Locarno in London, if not as big. It was difficult to be gainfully occupied for three Radio Officers during time in port. We did our best to keep out of harms 
2 CONC way, so to speak, by staying out of view. Our Chief was probably 12 years our senior and not given to the interests exhibited by the two younger guys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dancing was my forte, in a way. I met a young English girl who sang a couple songs as entertainment. Asking her to dance my accent usually aroused interest, if nothing else. I recall she assured me that even as she was born there,
2 CONC  she considered herself English, like both her parents, whose parents had come from England. Her name was Barbara. Sigvart, just as avid a dancer, also met an English girl. I almost told Barbara about the Anglo-Indians we had met 
2 CONC at Bombay but thought better of it... I complimented her on her singing and she allowed that she had auditioned, or been interviewed, for a singing position at a local radio station.
2 CONT
2 CONT The reason for this part of the story from Durban is that we thought one could perceive a possible undercurrent boding future change. Sigvart and I had noticed that native groups, apparently from the surrounding area, would be sig
2 CONC ht-seeing, especially on weekends. Their different mode of dress indicated they were from outside Durban. We tried not to "look" at the women whose bosom was covered, just by a netting-type halter. It was almost as if they were vi
2 CONC siting the city, "in their land". Sigvart and I also visited the Seamen’s Church and was invited to coffee with some Norwegian ladies meeting there regularly. Their husbands were with business firms in the city. They tried to fill
2 CONC  us in on their operations in the area; we were politely properly interested. One of the motormen, a couple of years older than Sigvart and I, always a good dresser, had talked to both of us about the engineers school at London. H
2 CONC e had adequate time in the engine room to qualify, was unsure whether he had enough machine shop time ashore. Because we were eventually bound for England, after South Africa and apparently Argentina, we encouraged him to make a g
2 CONC o for it. The Chief Engineer had assured him he had his support.
2 CONT
2 CONT Going shore on Sunday afternoon I met the motorman in company of an "English" woman with a boy and girl, about 12 and 10. He introduced me, they had been on an outing to a park outside the city where one could rent boat for excurs
2 CONC ion on a lake. After some small talk I went on to meet Barbara.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meeting the motorman onboard the next day he asked if I was surprised meeting him. He stated he had met her and a friend at the dance area Sigvart and I frequented. They had been keeping company for about a week or so. She was div
2 CONC orced and her former husband was in a hospital with spine damage which rendered him lame. Didn’t quite understand why he told me about this except to explain he was quite involved for the time being. He said they had talked about 
2 CONC her friend, who apparently was of "native" background, i.e. of Dutch and native mixture. He said he was surprised why she mentioned this; the two lived in the same apartment building and seemed to be together a lot. She was usuall
2 CONC y at the divorcee’s place when he called.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other motormen had chided him, for fun, about the late times he apparently spent at her house time or two. He told me she had mentioned she would be quite lonely again after we had to leave. He received a ribbing coming onboard qu
2 CONC ite early one morning. She and her children only had a two-room apartment with kitchen and bath; she supported herself as a seamstress with help from her former mother-in-law because her son had been a "wanderer".
2 CONT
2 CONT The motorman seemed to need someone to talk to apparently because he felt involved beyond the usual chance meeting. (I wasn’t quite acquainted with the term ‘confessional’, as yet). We had a good laugh together when I mentioned th
2 CONC at perhaps it would be better to sign on an oil tanker, with likely only 36 hours in port.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was invited to the Barbara’s home for tea, really! Let this slip to Sigvart and the Chief who told the other Officers. Even the Captain got involved; they were standing on deck as I left. The Chief Mate: -"you are going to a tea
2 CONC ?, -man, these radio people", but was in good fun. We had discharged the cargo and were tied to buoys in the harbor awaiting birth to take on coal cargo for Argentina. A launch took me ashore where Barbara picked me up in their ca
2 CONC r. They lived in a large home with a garden and two native servants on the outskirts of the city. Both she and her father worked in the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbara had a younger brother who was full of questions about Norway and the sea. I could not really understand how I had impressed these people that much, their hospitality not a usual "fare" for a Seaman. I was complimented on m
2 CONC y command of their language; perhaps this was part of the girl’s interest as well. I did, in a round about manner, manage to express an interest in the groups of the native people I had noticed on their apparent visits. -Would the
2 CONC re be possible future concerns regarding their progress; my thoughts, "it was their land after all", might, unintentionally’ have come through... The girl’s father thought I was quite perceptive. (I am sure I looked that word up l
2 CONC ater), at least getting the gist of it for the time being. But it was his opinion that, for the foreseeable future, it was not expected to become a problem. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The tea ended talking about sport differences in the two countries. I was impressed with the parents interest and knowledge and surmised their conversation also served as information and education for the younger brother. I contin
2 CONC ued to enjoy this girl’s company the rest of the time we were there. Later I was surprised to receive a letter from her, which reached me while at New York. I believe she mentioned disappointment over her failed auditions; any ans
2 CONC wer is a faded memory...
2 CONT
2 CONT Digression:...
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early fifties 1950s, settled in Brooklyn, N.Y., I recall wondering about this family when I read journalist Robert Ruark’s book, "Something of Value", about life in East Africa. It was about the burgeoning problems between 
2 CONC the native people and those of Dutch background, who mainly had developed the farming country. Ruark was a journalist for The New York Times. He also wrote in relation to, perhaps a book, the African word "UHURU",, (perhaps also a
2 CONC  movie), meaning freedom in one native tongue. I wondered, again, if it was that obvious to me, that I at 25 could perceive conditional, future, problems during a 5 weeks’ stay with a ship, with the present more on my mind... I re
2 CONC ally did not fancy myself that perceptive.
2 CONT
2 CONT At a library here in Raleigh, 1995, looking for information about this author, the librarian was ever so willing, and informed, about Ruark. He was a native of Wilmington, N.C., on the coast, just about 150 miles from our own home
2 CONC  in Raleigh. (-small, small world, after all?).
2 CONT
2 CONT But, Back to Durban:
2 CONT
2 CONT Braganza was moved to a coal pier to take on this cargo for Buenos Aires, Argentina. Sigvart and I just about found it necessary to seal our cabin against the coal dust. It seemed to take twice as long to get ready to go ashore a 
2 CONC couple of last times, to bid our good buys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigvart and I flipped a coin about choice of watch, the Chief sparks taking the eight to twelve watch. I got the four to eight watch which also happened to be our departure time, in the late afternoon. Preparing to heave up the ma
2 CONC in antenna, which had been disconnected because it interfered with the discharge and lading I found that one of the about foot long ceramic insulators had been broken. It had gone unnoticed because it was secured at the crow’s-nes
2 CONC t level at the main, (aft) mast. Obtaining a spare I was advised by the bosun that all his men were occupied making ready for open the sea, just outside the breakwater. I, then, became the next choice to replace the insulator. By 
2 CONC the time the ship was passing the breakwater I was sitting at the crowsnest level of the mast, my legs around the mast, working the weather-beaten, small, turn-bolts cutting in the new insulator.
2 CONT
2 CONT It happened on my watch, hence I ‘volunteered for the job as my duty. I distinctly remember holding on as the ship was taken by the sea, once beyond the breakwater, I could just about look straight down into the sea, I thought, as
2 CONC  the ship leaned over, now being a part of the most leaning about 75 feet above the deck. Most of all I remember, distinctly too, that after I had secured the antenna, hoisted it in place between the two masts and returned to the 
2 CONC radio station, I had forgotten to be sea sick. Too occupied with an important job, I mused, thinking maybe a little nerve... nah, not this Seaman! The bosun- even complimented me to our Chief; I had earned the respect of the deck 
2 CONC crew. (I felt good even as I knew Sigvart would just have taken it in stride)...
2 CONT
2 CONT We sailed south, then westward past Cape Agulhas at the southern-most tip of South Africa. We were contacted by signal light, flipping the lightbeam down and up, the Morse code letters formed in the down position, from the top of 
2 CONC a mountain as we moved on past the Cape of Good Hope after dark. It was a mere name reporting procedure and we sailed on into the night, westward. That sounds almost romantic, I thought, but it was just the beginning of a two week
2 CONC  plus long trip across the open South Atlantic.
2 CONT
2 CONT About half way to Buenos Aires our Steward came to our little mess room at noon, one day, advising that our meal would be a little late. The cook had reported to him that he had a little trouble with one of the Africaner mess men.
2 CONC  The particular individual had complained that some of the work he had to do, as part of his job, was like ‘slave work’. I tried to be jovial stating I had done that type of work, maybe I could talk to him. The Steward reminded me
2 CONC  I was an Officer now, only half smiling, and that he would handle it his way. He proceeded to have a talk-to-meeting with the young Africaner outside the galley, reminding him in his limited English: -"we no have slaves in Norway
2 CONC , we do all work ourselves; only you people have slaves!". Somehow this English was adequate and seemed to get through to the Africaner. Work resumed.
2 CONT
2 CONT During a later talk with the messman he informed me he knew about Norwegian whaling ships having called at Walvis Bay in the now Namibia, formerly South West Africa. I was unable to ascertain if that had been his home and how long
2 CONC  he had been sailing as a messman. He did not mention the incident with the Steward. For me it was somewhat of a learning curve about the conditions in this land of the Boers and General Jan Christian Smuts, the mixture of peoples
2 CONC  that nature seemed to leave behind, one way or another.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ventilator incident:
2 CONT
2 CONT A couple of days before we were due at Buenos Aires we began to pick up the local radio stations and the then lilting type of music, for first timers Sigvart and I, anyway. (Which same music was to become more ceaseless and unendi
2 CONC ng, almost boring, before we were due to leave). Coming off watch in the dark, at eight, or 2000hrs, the habit of calisthenics led to a sort of collision. A capped ventilator was situated on the deck in the mid-ship area which I h
2 CONC ad walked by every day. Bending from the waist a collision occurred between my face and a sharp corner of one of several square nuts fastening the cover of this capped ventilator. (When describing this incident to an acquaintance,
2 CONC  probably 25 years later, it was, "humorously?", called the collision between two nuts because I was aware of the ventilator, just failed to pay attention).
2 CONT
2 CONT But it was not very funny that night. My lower lip was pierced and several front teeth were loose. It was a mess. By morning, though, I had most of it cleaned up, appearing almost no worse. The next day the keel of the ship appare
2 CONC ntly contacted a sand bank and the momentary contact almost threw standing individuals to the deck. The neophytes were told its was not an unusual happening at the mouth of the La Plata river. And because there was no talk of seek
2 CONC ing drydock to assess possible damage to the keel plates the happening was taken as explained. One just acted as if it were part what one had to learn about sea-life..
2 CONT
2 CONT The shipping agent made appointment for me with a dentist whose father had emigrated from Norway, and was now in the advertising business. I became acquainted with the families, the dentist was interested to have his father meet m
2 CONC e. But their social environs and interest generally were far different from a Seaman’s. I was able to attend a couple of tennis matches because the dentist was a top class player in Buenos Aires. It was generally difficult to asso
2 CONC ciate where some individuals were not too conversant with English. The dentist had a brother just about my age. They all spoke English without any problem.
2 CONT
2 CONT The damaged teeth were repaired, eventually necessitating porcelain caps years later in the U.S. (A partial upper, front two teeth and failed molars, became necessary 45 years after the ‘nut’ incident, the bone structure apparentl
2 CONC y failing to hold the ‘pegs’ for the caps).
2 CONT
2 CONT The coal was unloaded at an electric power generating station. We were told that incoming train loads of grain was being mixed with the coal because of the country’s ample grain supply and apparent export difficulty due to the war
2 CONC . The life in and around this metropolis was new experience for Sigvart and I. Buenos Aires was famous, or infamous, for an esplanade type area with all types of entertainment. I made my way to the to the opera house, or building,
2 CONC  either because it was rumored as an exceptionally large building, or possibly to a take in an opera. As has been noted before time in port is not a busy time for a Radio Officer, let alone three on the same ship. Consequently, I 
2 CONC was out roaming again, often just riding streetcars or walking about.
2 CONT
2 CONT Neither Sigvart nor I were that interested in the availability of the renowned night life. Not that we were disinterested either, as the saying goes. But six weeks in a port takes a lot of money, just to make the time pass... At t
2 CONC imes the night watchman would come and turn off our cabin lights, both of us having fallen asleep trying to read into the night.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was a rather well equipped Seamen’s church because of the many visits by Norwegian ships. A Norwegian couple served as caretakers. They had two children who attended local schools and apparently were born there. The parents 
2 CONC explained that their children were Argentine citizens. I was not sure whether he was an ordained minister. The Steward and I spent some time there and were invited to the couple’s quarters. For me the two were a source of informat
2 CONC ion about life there generally. I was especially interested in a part of the life Sigvart and I had experienced in our wanderings around town in the evenings.
2 CONT
2 CONT It seemed that every restaurant and dancing area had at least one young lady engaged in singing with the orchestra. I wondered about these young ladies, if they were aspiring to develop a career in singing. The wife told us most o
2 CONC f them likely would not go very far in that career and probably would end up ‘working’ downtown if they persisted trying. Sadly, she would state, opportunities for young women were scarce. She made note of my interest in the socia
2 CONC l mores, also mentioning young women’s difficult conditions trying to make a go in the business world. It seemed a given that a young girl’s progress depended on "accommodation" with the owner or manager; which in itself may not h
2 CONC ave been unusual. In the ‘North, though a girl or woman could have an option of jobs elsewhere.
2 CONT
2 CONT This was a different world for Sigvart and I coming from small towns in Norway. (I seem to remember that there were no women working in the office for the Carbide factory in Odda). It was a growing and learning period continued fr
2 CONC om London which was my first experience with big city life. I mentioned my experience with the Arab farmer wishing to sell his daughter, literally dragging her out from the tent. The minister’s wife noted that she was glad they ha
2 CONC d two sons and that maybe the improvement, as to a young woman’s lot, was less than it appeared to be even in this developed metropolis. The couple did not seem to have a wide circle of friends. Once I thought I spotted the Stewar
2 CONC d on his way to their quarters when the minister was otherwise occupied visiting ships; I let this slip by, however...
2 CONT
2 CONT I enjoyed the Steward’s company. Over coffee he would boast having worked as a cook on British fishing vessels, before the war, as they often would fish illegally in Norwegian waters. He countered this with Norwegian fishing vesse
2 CONC ls doing the same in other waters. I was surprised at his limited facility with the English language having served on the fishing vessels. He was a wealth of information. I was all ears as he and Sigvart would ‘discourse’ on fishi
2 CONC ng life generally.
2 CONT
2 CONT I did not ask if he had visited the ‘parsonage’, as alluded to above. He did however let slip that he thought he had observed their was some distance between the two in that house. I let it pass as something outside my ken, possib
2 CONC le friction between married people. The fact he would include me in those thought was as if he intended to reveal what really was not my concern.
2 CONT
2 CONT He likely was in his fifties, always pleasant, and I was pleased that he would take the time to accompany me ashore. It was as if he took pains to spend more time with our group than with the rest of the Officers. During supper on
2 CONC e day Sigvart noted it seemed I was always on the go ashore. "Was there something special?". My –"just wished to make the most of this visit because I didn’t really expect to come back", was barely acceptable. As soon as the war w
2 CONC as over so would be my sea life. "You haven’t seen me seasick yet"? But they both thought that was no problem. The Chief had noted my restlessness, thought I would grow out of it, I’d settle down. "Ashore", I retorted and we all l
2 CONC aughed. Visiting parks and museums was more to while away time than a drive for discovery; museums generally amount to a look backward... Sigvart often would keep me company. But the Chief said he didn’t need all that exercise.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Second Mate, Ole Moen, was from ‘outer’ Hardanger, near where my father came from. He and the other Officers had fun hazing me about my goings and comings, alluding to possible clandestine amorous affairs. Our Chief stated I w
2 CONC as mostly roaming, -did he know?. But it was a good feeling they would take the time pull my chain’, even as that expression would have meant nothing that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Acting like I was in training to become a world traveler, I was ever aware of the fact that I was in company, privileged at that, of people whose chosen life was beyond my perception or understanding, in a sense. I had a sense of 
2 CONC respect for their lifestyle, so chosen or one of necessity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Trying in a sense to become a world traveler I became almost acutely aware of my Spanish shortcoming. I thought it was the "in" thing when I was learning French; Spanish, I found out, was almost as universal as English, but that w
2 CONC as water under the bridge now... As a foreigner one remains more so without at least some knowledge of the native language,, no matter how willing people should be to speak a common tongue.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the engineers had sailed to this city before the war. He wished to visit a couple of former ‘German bars/restaurants. I could not really see the interest in this but went along, as if for a change of venue. The first bar wa
2 CONC s almost empty and it seemed that the people there knew at once we were just being nosy; tension begetting tension... Argentina by this time had declared for the Allies, though apparently not as a combatant nation. As the drinks w
2 CONC ere ordered there was some low discussion going on at the bar, the Norwegians sitting at the tables near the exit as if they didn’t care for even cursory acquaintance?.
2 CONT
2 CONT The atmosphere just seemed awkward even as the Seamen tried to act casually. We left without reordering as if to show we were just on a look-see expedition. There was no overt feeling of enmity towards these former Germans. But th
2 CONC ere was no interest making their acquaintance either...
2 CONT
2 CONT The second bar had a restaurant and the engineer who had been there before stated he wished to sample some of their formerly renowned food. But the others made it a no-show for food, one drink a piece to show their disinterest. I 
2 CONC just felt we had come to be nosy and to see how the former "Herrenvolk’ now fared; I pitied them, in a way. It was obvious by this time that the tide had begun to turn against Hitler and it served no purpose reminding these people
2 CONC , rubbing it in, as it were. The one drink order seemed reminder enough of whatever we were trying to prove.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bored in Buenos Aires?..
2 CONT
2 CONT During the last phase of our lading operation time was starting to hang heavy’. Tedium, or plain boredom, either expression seemed to run counter to the possibilities for diversion in this metropolis, yet neither far fetched. Eith
2 CONC er could serve as a title for the next episode.
2 CONT
2 CONT We had come from a long stay in Bombay; we had a considerable stay at Durban. In about a month’s time we were likely to arrive back in England. A year, almost, would have passed since we left for India. The memory of the girl I ha
2 CONC d known at London had almost been shoved into the background... It had also crossed my mind that after this ‘round trip’ I ought to be able to get ‘back to New York, to visit my brother and Uncle. The regular crew, it seemed, beha
2 CONC ved almost as if they were at home, which in a sense they were; which in a sense made me the odd-ball. Sigvart had been at sea for some time. The Chief, our Chief, would join us at times to talk about some of his interests, which 
2 CONC included politics. Sigvart and I let this slide, which was unusual for me; I did wonder if the Chief were attempting recruiting.... The two had been at sea before the war. The Chief was from northern Norway, some place whose name 
2 CONC I forgot. Sigvart was from northwestern Norway, the coastal city of Kristiansund, not too far from my mother’s home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigvart and I had gone to Radio Officer school together at London. Both of us had noticed, and run across, a slight bias against "war-time" trained Officers. (Months later in the U.S. we would encounter the expression, ‘90 day Won
2 CONC ders’, in reference to Merchant Marine Officers receiving a three month course to become Officers). Our Chief felt it would pass with time, depending on our attitudes and behavior.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is noteworthy that the deck and engine Officers trained at London had less of a problem, if that is what it was; all of them were former Seamen with required time at sea. There was no required time at sea in order to become a R
2 CONC adio Officer. Indeed, before the war, it was not unusual for some who had failed at the university to go to radio school and become Seamen. Our Chief felt we should be ready to accept that some regular Officers, and Seamen as well
2 CONC , might differentiate between ‘regular’ Seamen and the newer crop of Radio Officers. It was almost as if a different skill level, electronics, caused some difference. (As noted before during the depression 2nd and 3rd deck Officer
2 CONC s would obtain a radio license in order to get a job, because the Radio Officer could be part time, by international agreement).
2 CONT
2 CONT The position of the electrician as a member of the engine Officers was established, especially since the advent of the electric winch systems. It would, then, be a learning experience, a time of adaptation. In fact, even though we
2 CONC  were quartered with the Officers and took our meals at the Officers mess, regular Officer status was generally not accorded the telegrapher before the war on Scandinavian ships. The advent of the war thus acted as an accelerator,
2 CONC  in a sense.
2 CONT
2 CONT The cargo received at Buenos Aires consisted of about 4000 tons of linseed, (I was unable to learn if the oil made from the seeds had a special war-time value), and 6000 tons of canned meats for England. We traveled up the La Plat
2 CONC a river to Rosario to complete the lading. The Chief Mate called on Sigvart to take the wheel to Rosario because the Argentine river pilot was dissatisfied with the regular A.Bs’ wheel handling. The sailing took place at night wit
2 CONC h the express need to hold the ship’s course true exactly from the beam zone of one lighthouse light beam, on one side of the river, toward the zone of the next lighthouse beam on the other side of the river. Rosario is a much sma
2 CONC ller city. There was no particular interest about the cargo and the shore-time was almost "deja vu".
2 CONT
2 CONT Leaving Rosario Sigvart was called to the bridge to handle the wheel again on the way ‘down’ the river, even as it was in the daytime, till we had passed by Buenos Aires. This, in itself, was highly unusual that none of the AB’s w
2 CONC ere entrusted with the wheel up and down the river; perhaps a wartime circumstance)... Needless to say, there was a an attitude change, Seamen to Radio Officers, after these two wheel episodes.
2 CONT
2 CONT We were to stop at Montevideo, Uruguay, on the north side of the La Plata river. In addition to more cargo we also ‘picked up’ four former whalers as passengers. They were from an earlier Norwegian whaling operation discontinued b
2 CONC ecause of the war, apparently. (As an aside, only one of these four survived our lifeboat experience). Liberty, in Navy talk, means any free time to go ashore. But for a radio man, with almost no duty in port Montevideo was just a
2 CONC nother place after all the ‘liberty in Argentina. Teaming up with the engine people, the fourth engineer was my age, we visited some of the customary hangouts, or watering holes. The fourth engineer and I were treated to a very sc
2 CONC antily clad young girl doing a table dance for us at the owner’s urging one evening. Another young girl joined us and the owner said –"acompanar" and hotel, pointing to the two girls. The possible problem with "VD" likely cooled t
2 CONC hat possible event...
2 CONT
2 CONT I thought I perceived a difference in attitude, almost a reserved demeanor on the part of the locals ; we were at war, these people somehow wished us to know they were neutral. This became quite obvious on a trip shore by myself, 
2 CONC one afternoon. Protesting receiving the wrong change after a meal the proprietor suddenly spoke no English, repeating –"engles, engles", even as I protested "norte" and "noruega" in my only Spanish. The customers fell silent and s
2 CONC tudied me ominously as the bartender went through the motions of recounting the money in the cash register. Receiving the correct change I mumbled ‘grazia’ and hurriedly left the unfinished drink and the area.
2 CONT
2 CONT We had been advised it was not really smart to be walking around alone at night. The fourth engineer and I were indeed followed by a threesome of young guys one night but as we stepped up our pace they seemed to lose interest. A c
2 CONC rew member in company of a local girl left alone after being called Inglese by a couple of guys seemingly bent on separating the two. There were no reports about anyone being harmed. Remembering walking through the native quarters
2 CONC  at Casablanca, any time, I wondered briefly about the difference.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally taking our leave of Montevideo we passed by the buoys, at about the three mile limit from the coast line, marking the area where the German "pocket" battleship "Graf Spee" was scuttled. The Graf Spee had operated as a raid
2 CONC er against allied shipping in the South Atlantic and Indian oceans. She had been tracked down and damaged by the British Navy and forced to seek harbor at Montevideo. Uruguay, a neutral country, could only allow the war ship 72 ho
2 CONC urs for repairs or intern her according to international law.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the eighties I remember a replay in a TV documentary about the permission from Hitler to the Captain of the ‘Graf Spee’ to scuttle the ship. The documentary seemed to indicate the British had been able to convince the Germa
2 CONC ns they would have enough ships available to turn any battle against the Graf Spee. It was some satisfaction for us to pass by the ‘,remains" of the German enemy, defeated and scuttled ingloriously. Even so, admittedly, she had be
2 CONC en a formidable class of a man of war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leaving Montevideo our orders were to travel alone to Freetown, Sierra Leone, in West Africa, and apparently wait for and join a convoy for England, the destination of our cargo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Go to: Chapter Seventeen -
2 CONT
2 CONT BRAGANZA FOUNDERS; Lifeboat Story
2 CONT
2 CONT IndexBRAGANZA FOUNDERS; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lifeboat Story
2 CONT
2 CONT The Fire:
2 CONT
2 CONT Two days after leaving Montevideo I was on watch, the 1200 hours to 1600 hours. The absence of the regular thump-thump from the diesel engine, or motor, signaled that it had stopped. As noted before, the motion of a ship changes m
2 CONC arkedly when she is no longer under power. After a little while, because these stops were almost a daily routine, I glanced out the rear porthole and was surprised to see the Second Engineer on the boat deck with a hand held water
2 CONC  fire extinguisher. He was literally squirting water on a fire blazing up from the skylight opening, on a wooden "chicken coop" so called, built to allow the skylights to remain open during the night while blocking the light from 
2 CONC the engine room. I thought briefly it seemed a futile effort. I learned later that the fire in the engine room started when an oil line apparently burst near an exhaust pipe, the hot exhaust pipe igniting the oil, causing the fire
2 CONC  to spread rapidly through the engine room.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moving out to the bridge, concerned now about my first duty, possibly to send out an SOS; nobody was there. Going below to the Captain’s quarters Captain Gaulen, over 60, was packing papers, etc. The obvious condition prompted me 
2 CONC to inquire: "Do you want me to send out an SOS?". The Captain looked up stating, matter-of-factly, almost: -"we might as well".
2 CONT
2 CONT When I advised there was no one on the bridge to confirm our position the Captain just looked up, ordering: -"then you go to the chart room and find it yourself!".
2 CONT
2 CONT I was now vaguely aware of being in a situation for which Radio Officers really are necessary on a ship. The training for the condition now at hand had been from the teachers at the radio school, all former Radio Officers, who tau
2 CONC ght from experience or general knowledge. My action seemed as if by rote as I composed the SOS message, from memory, with the longitude and latitude information obtained from the latest position at noon from the chart room, with t
2 CONC he name of the ship, ending with "Master".
2 CONT
2 CONT My last contact with anybody else was the ‘old man’s order to find the position in the chart room. on the way there I had noticed two life boats off at a distance, but did not learn about that somewhat precarious situation till la
2 CONC ter. (Because of the approaching flames some of the crew, 3 or 4 to a boat, had lowered the two starboard lifeboats and were unable to handle them in the seas as they drifted away from the ship. The Second Mate had been able to ge
2 CONC t enough people in the wooden port-side lifeboat, which also had been on fire, and bring the two other boats back; which likely also is the reason I am able to relate this story.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Starting the transmitter I became aware of a noise from the boat-deck. Glancing out the porthole I saw the wooden chicken coop structure fall into the engine room in a blaze. The power apparently failed about the same because the 
2 CONC transmitter "died". This may have been coincidental but it crossed my mind that perhaps the burning wood fell on the generator.
2 CONT
2 CONT The emergency transmitter was a battery operated spark gap transmitter, (hence the Radio Officer moniker "Sparks"). It was designed mostly as an emergency device and inherently limited. Starting it up and moving the switch in the 
2 CONC ceiling to couple this transmitter to the main antenna I keyed out the emergency message. The noise from the spark gap, as the voltage arced across the "gap" to the crystal, focused my attention on the other necessary job at hand,
2 CONC  once the SOS message was transmitted. The code books were kept in a weighted, perforated, steel box. I had mentioned the code box to the Captain and his orders, peculiarly I thought then, were to bring it to the lifeboat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heading out to the bridge to get down to the main deck, in order to get forward to the bow expecting the lifeboats waiting for me below the bow became a rude awakening. The usual way down was "blocked" because the fire had reached
2 CONC  the starboard pill box for the Oerlicon 20mm antiaircraft gun on the boat deck. A veritable barrage of exploding ammunition, (it seemed so!) -indicated by tracer bullets sprayed in all directions. Fortunately, it crossed my mind,
2 CONC  keeping low I found enough rope to first lower the code box, then myself over the front of the bridge down to the main deck, three decks in all.
2 CONT
2 CONT I was vaguely aware of moving almost at an unhurried yet steady pace, as if by a plan. Proceeding towards the bow, oddly being aware of being on the starboard side, making my way past the two forward hatches, maybe some 120 feet i
2 CONC n all, the deck now "moving" some in the rising seas. I was aware, too, of being all alone even reasoning that the job of getting into the lifeboats likely had occupied everyone because of the approaching fire that finally chased 
2 CONC me over the front of the bridge. (I cannot recall if I considered trying the other side of the bridge to the deck; apparently the rope offered an extant, immediate, solution).
2 CONT
2 CONT I remember being surprised, too, that the Chief Radio Officer had not paid me a visit during the time I was occupied in the radio shack, (with the "last rites"‘.?). Sigvart, the other Radio Officer, I learned later, had been rathe
2 CONC r actively involved with first putting out a fire in the only wooden lifeboat, on the port side, getting the boat into the sea and bringing the two other boats back. The Chief likely had been occupied aiding the Captain getting in
2 CONC to the lifeboat. The usual romantic notion of the Captain being the last to leave the ship, is just that. our Captain was about 60, now having to get down a rope ladder from the deck railing into a lifeboat about 30 feet below, ri
2 CONC ding on the rising and falling seas in an approaching storm, fire blazing 50 feet away. Trying to step into the lifeboat too soon easily could break a leg, too late could result in a free fall, four to five feet, into a descending
2 CONC  boat. The Chief Engineer was also about the same age and rather obese.
2 CONT
2 CONT Continuing forward towards the bow I noticed the swamped motorboat some distance away, which motorboat was supposed to tow the lifeboats. It crossed my mind, sort of matter-of-factly, that the motorboat was more suited to chug aro
2 CONC und in a fjord than being out in open sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Captain’s lifeboat was waiting below the port bow area, being the lee side. They motioned for me to lower the code box, then myself into the boat. I had not had time to ask the Captain why he wanted the code box brought into t
2 CONC he lifeboat but raised the issue now that should a German sub surface they would be looking for the box. Before he could answer I resolutely heaved it over the side. Watching it sink, as designed, the Captain nodding his head indi
2 CONC cating "OK ".
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chief Radio Officer also was in the Captain’s boat. He decided to climb back onboard for a last "look-see". The ammunition no longer being sprayed about he was able to enter the bridge. Returning, he transferred to the Chief M
2 CONC ate’s boat. The fire was spreading rapidly stern-ward from the boat deck. It seemed evident the fire was reaching the linseed cargo in the aft cargo holds by way of the oil in the bilges.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two lifeboats soon became separated because of the high seas. Sigvart’s wooden lifeboat, with 4 AB’s already had moved out of view. Meeting again ashore Sigvart related how they were able to set sail right away. The wooden lif
2 CONC e boats generally were built more to sail, with a deeper v-cut bottom and a 5-6 inch keel. The steel boats were more flat-bottomed and generally able to sail only with the wind. The Captain’s boat had 26 people including half the 
2 CONC English gun crew.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of the steadily increasing seas the Second Mate ordered the boat turned into the seas and that two drift, or sea, anchors be deployed. They are made up of a three and a half foot diameter, ‘hoola-hoop’ type, steel ring wit
2 CONC h a long canvas cone attached, open at the end. Fastened to an about 100 ft long lanyard it will sink and serve as a drag anchor as the boat drifts with the sea, holding the bow into the seas to prevent the boat from broaching and
2 CONC  capsizing. Everyone started huddling under a couple of tarpaulins as the inclement weather increased, except a Swedish AB and the old Steward who had spent a lot of time on fishing boats. During the approaching evening and throug
2 CONC h the night one could see the two standing in the bow tending to the lines for the sea anchors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once ashore we learned that the Chief Mate’s boat was to lose three of the passengers that joined at Montevideo. Their main problem, in addition to exposure, was that they had been ashore so long, consuming amounts of cheap alcoho
2 CONC l, "puro", that when alcohol was no longer available to satisfy their body system’s cravings, they could no longer endure the rigors.
2 CONT
2 CONT (I had heard that in fact they had brought onboard an about 30 gallon glass container enclosed in wicker for the trip to England. The Chief Mate stated later just one bottle of that would likely have kept them alive in the lifeboa
2 CONC t). one of them, an older man, was just found dead after first night. The other two, one by one, passed through stages of delirium tremens before succumbing. The three were "buried", by committing them to the sea, after removing t
2 CONC heir clothes, the Second Engineer doing the duty of a parson, short form.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole, the Second Mate, was in charge of our boat and made me his second in command. The Captain agreed to this. Ole and I decided to stand two hour watches, occupying the aft compartment. The seas were breaking over the bow constan
2 CONC tly and bailing became a continuous necessity. Taking the first turn in the darkness, a thought about the Lord’s prayer momentarily crossed my mind in the dark, but just as quickly returning to the necessary continuous bailing and
2 CONC  my duty to the rest of the crew.
2 CONT
2 CONT Midnight approaching I noticed the water level seemed to be increasing. It was about Ole’s turn, anyway, and I nudged him so as not to cause undue stir, whispering about the water level, as if anyone would notice over the din of t
2 CONC he storm in the pitch darkness. Lifting the floor board we found that the screw cap over the hole used to empty the boat, when heaved out of the water, had been knocked loose by a small piece of board floating back and forth, the 
2 CONC sea just streaming into the boat. I quickly knotted the handkerChief, (see, even sailors use these), plugging the hole. As an aside, a rubber ball in a basket fixture outside the tube, or port, was supposed to prevent just this co
2 CONC ndition. Apparently it had just rotted away during the stay at Bombay and not checked before leaving... Plugging the hole I thought the incident funny and laughed. Ole grabbed me thinking I was near panic and about to backhand me 
2 CONC for a little shock treatment; I assured Ole it was OK, just found it funny. Ole answered: -"you’re crazy, like all Sparks ". But I thought I noticed Ole found it funny, too.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ole roused me some time later because someone was signaling, lying low in the seas, by flipping a search light up and down, likely locating us by the lantern on our mast. It was a submarine asking our name. The sub disappeared as 
2 CONC soon as I complied. We reasoned it was a German waiting for us. Buenos Aires was a known German spy beehive. (We also wondered, to ourselves, if the sub would reappear come daylight; sinking life boats with machine-gun fire by the
2 CONC  new breed German sub Captains was not uncommon. By contrast, early in the war some apparently old line German sub Captains would surface and aid the crews in the lifeboats, even towing boats toward the coast). Once ashore Sigvart
2 CONC  related the Braganza had blown up in a fireball around midnight, which also could have alerted the Germans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Waking Ole before dawn broke he came out of a deep sleep: -"my legs are gone!", he uttered, his legs too numb to move from sitting in a crouch, partly in cold water. I had to assure him he was OK and helped him move around ‘till h
2 CONC e ‘found’ his legs again. This was in October, same as March, northern time frame. Waking up later I had to hold my jaws thinking my teeth would break from excessive shivering. The waves were unbelievingly high with the boat as if
2 CONC  racing down into the trough from the crest, like on a roller coaster, being held bow ahead by the sea anchors. One wondered if the bow would stay clear, ("are we going to make it?"), as it climbed up the next wave. The size of th
2 CONC e waves may be estimated by the about 20 foot boat ‘sailing’ down into the trough, the lanyards for the sea anchors taughtely ahead. Ole complimented the Swedish AB and the old Steward for watching the lanyards during the long nig
2 CONC ht, which had kept the boat from capsizing; everyone agreed...
2 CONT
2 CONT The storm was abating as the day wore on and everyone was trying to dry out. The Captain delegated Ole and I to be in charge of rationing food and water. Both the Captain and the Chief Engineer were showing signs of suffering from
2 CONC  the rigors. There were two barrels of water, about 40 gallons each, rationed to about 6 oz. twice a day; several boxes of canned, ready-to-eat, high protein foods, (Pemican?), most of which made me sick because of fat content. (I
2 CONC  have to admit that during the nightwatch I would sneak an extra measure of water, reasoning it was deserved... )
2 CONT
2 CONT Two flotation tanks along each side of the boat, under the seats running from stern to bow, were filled with hard tacks, each the size of half a hamburger roll. (-a hard tack is like a zwieback consistency). Each tank had two 7 in
2 CONC ch screw covers with rubber gaskets. Filling the tanks with hard tacks one would light two candles in each tank, tighten the covers, and as the flames consumed all the oxygen the hard tacks would keep. (Apparently I also was too o
2 CONC ccupied to be bothered by sea sickness).
2 CONT
2 CONT The sail was rigged to take advantage of the wind. I rigged up the life boat transmitter antenna. The transmitter was powered by a generator cranked by two men. Several messages were sent to no apparent avail.
2 CONT
2 CONT The third day, now rowing mostly, brought three separate events. First a large cargo ship in ballast passed in a southwesterly direction. Ole put me in charge of the flare gun; several flares were fired. We figured it was impossib
2 CONC le that the flares were not seen because we were near enough to see that the ship was in ballast. But she just moved away and out of sight. One small incident took place because of an AB, a tall powerful Maltese, rowed more in a s
2 CONC taccato fashion of short strokes. Ole asked him to stay in tempo with the others used to rowing in longer, slower strokes.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second event was the appearance of two large sharks. We learned, after reaching shore and the rest of the crew, the reason for the sharks likely was the three men buried from the Chief Mate’s boat. I thought one shark came too
2 CONC  close and stuck my oar into it; it just moved out of reach.
2 CONT
2 CONT The third event was the fourth passenger sailor from Montevideo who had to be quieted down, physically, as he verbally attacked the Captain for not having "picked a better boat". He likely was suffering the after-effects of a prol
2 CONC onged drinking period at Montevideo, already noted. Being younger and in better condition than the others in the Chief Mate’s boat he survived the ordeal, including necessarily being physically subdued and tied up most of the day.
2 CONC  The incident brought back memories about my friend Oscar buried in Algiers...
2 CONT
2 CONT The next night was moonlit. I thought of noting, romantic-wise, we were sailing by the Southern Cross, but thought better of it. It may be noted here, too, that the Chief Mate’s crew were picked up by a passing freighter and broug
2 CONC ht to Rio de Janeiro. Sigvart and the four A.B.s sailed their wooden lifeboat to shore in three and a half days. They were all experienced Seamen. Sigvart explained that the wooden boat was just light enough, with just five men, t
2 CONC o be lifted to the crest of the waves, moving with the sea. Whenever a crest threatened to break over the stern hitting the wave crest with an oar would sort of "split" it he explained. Sigvart hailed from Kristiansund, northweste
2 CONC rn coast of Norway, and well versed in the operation of boats. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Late in the afternoon the fifth day some of the crew thought they could see land. The Captain and Ole disagreed but Ole brought out the leaded ‘"sounding" line anyway; several throws of the line and lead had no results. Towards ev
2 CONC ening of the next day we spotted a light house. Ole and the Captain agreed this would be in northern Uruguay, a neutral country, hence a place to avoid so as not to be interned as members of a warring nation, (international law). 
2 CONC We kept on rowing north along the coast-line, staying well clear, through the night into the middle of the day. About mid-morning the mast tops of a southbound ship became visible to the east of us, by about a 100 yards. our flare
2 CONC s were used up and the radio water damaged. It was likely a coastal steamer, the sea too high for them to see us.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just after noon we were likely about 6-700 yards off the beach. The Captain asked me how we were going get through the breakers, about 7 foot high, enough to capsize the boat should it broach. (I had already figured I could swim t
2 CONC he distance, in the worst case; self preservation to the fore?). The Captain turned to me because Ole now was completely exhausted, we were worried about whether he would make it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sea Promotion?;
2 CONT
2 CONT Surprising myself, (even thinking about it in later years), in a matter-of-fact sense telling the Captain I would take the boat in; with two sets of oars, two men per oar spaced one oarlock apart, for maneuvering, the men to follo
2 CONC w my orders exactly. The "old man" stood up in the boat stating Sparks was now in command of the boat, his orders to be followed. He also noted I had his, the Captain’s ‘38’, which he gave to me the first night in the lifeboat. (A
2 CONC sking why, he stated the crew might reason he had the gun, would not suspect me. I felt good about this trust, I thought... )
2 CONT
2 CONT It seemed redundant telling the rowers what to do: row as hard as they could till the boat would answer the tiller as I would turn towards the beach. Should the boat yaw the rowers on the "lee" side would hold while the rowers to 
2 CONC "windward" would lay harder on the oars. The powerful Maltese was one of the rowers and I counted cadence to be sure they were all "in stroke". The Captain was holding my leg as I stood bracing myself against the little poop deck,
2 CONC  the tiller between my legs. They understood at once and together we brought the boat straight up onto the beach, the bow digging into the sand. Some of the crew jumped ashore but I ordered that they help the old man, the Chief En
2 CONC gineer, and Ole ashore first. Continuing to order that we secure the boat by pulling it up the beach, as far as possible and bring the provisions ashore, I wondered where all this "ordering" came from, as if something I was wont t
2 CONC o do. The Captain who had been watching a few paces away now came over shaking my hand stating it was a job well done. Thanking him I felt it was the highest praise I could receive. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (As an aside, perhaps, that also ended my "sea promotion" to be in charge!).
2 CONT
2 CONT Abiding Luck?...
2 CONT
2 CONT A bus came by later using the hard beach as a roadway between the town of Rio Grande and Uruguay. The driver said he would advise authorities. Sigvart’s boat, landing in this area two days before us, had alerted authorities to wat
2 CONC ch out for two more boats. We were able to make a fire, stretch out, enjoy the food and water, savoring being ashore. We were picked up by an Army lorry towards midnight. The Soldiers picked up the lifeboat transmitter and also Ca
2 CONC ptain’s revolver which I proffered. We were transported to and quartered in the town, (in Rio Grande du Sol province), meeting Sigvart and the other four. We learned that the Chief Mate’s crew, picked up by a freighter, had been l
2 CONC anded at Rio de Janeiro.
2 CONT
2 CONT We needed a few days to settle down, clean our clothes, etc. Sigvart and I made the rounds of the local hotspots during the day as well as in the evenings. We were somewhat surprised to find there were bordellos, discussing this o
2 CONC ver a beer in a regular bar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Somehow both of us had relegated this type entertainment as belonging in France. But because neither of us had visited Brazil before we accepted it was a seaport town, hence maybe a custom. I should have remembered that a crewman 
2 CONC on the Tana, in North Africa, had owned a farm in the province west of Natal. He left to fight for Norway, didn’t know if he would return to Brazil. But he often talked about some of the customs among local farm families there. I 
2 CONC remember I admired his fluency in Portuguese and Spanish. It was not unusual, he stated, that young farm boys were initiated to the charms of the women at these houses, with full knowledge and acquiescence of their fathers. Sigvar
2 CONC t and I discussed, among sundry, how this war had thrown us together, taking us here and there, now finding ourselves in a small town in southern Brazil contemplating local mores.... (customs, more familiar that time).
2 CONT
2 CONT After a few days we boarded a c
